WEBVTT

00:00:06.958 --> 00:00:10.637
A campus sign can describe a room or a course through

00:00:10.637 --> 00:00:12.095
one shared operation.

00:00:12.095 --> 00:00:15.704
An interface names that contract, leaving each class

00:00:15.704 --> 00:00:17.231
to supply the details.

00:00:17.231 --> 00:00:19.383
This separation is abstraction.

00:00:44.161 --> 00:00:47.711
Labelled declares a public label operation without a

00:00:47.711 --> 00:00:48.052
body.

00:00:48.052 --> 00:00:51.261
Each class uses implements and supplies its own

00:00:51.261 --> 00:00:52.831
matching public method.

00:00:52.831 --> 00:00:56.244
The Override annotation asks the compiler to check

00:00:56.244 --> 00:00:56.995
that match.

00:00:56.995 --> 00:01:00.476
A default method includes a body that these classes

00:01:00.476 --> 00:01:00.886
share.

00:01:01.193 --> 00:01:04.933
The array holds Labelled references to different kinds

00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:05.695
of objects.

00:01:05.695 --> 00:01:09.228
The same announcement call uses each object's label

00:01:09.228 --> 00:01:10.267
implementation.

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.722
That is polymorphism.

00:01:11.722 --> 00:01:15.393
Predict both output lines in array order, then pause.

00:01:20.655 --> 00:01:23.580
The first announcement names Room Lab A.

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.920
The second names Course CSC-239.

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:29.723
Both begin with Ready because they share the default

00:01:29.723 --> 00:01:30.235
method.

00:01:30.235 --> 00:01:33.599
Each call inside that method reaches the label

00:01:33.599 --> 00:01:36.305
implementation of the current object.

00:01:36.380 --> 00:01:39.354
Change the room's constructor argument to Lab B.

00:01:39.354 --> 00:01:41.523
Predict which announcement changes.

00:01:41.523 --> 00:01:44.559
Why can the same loop still announce both objects

00:01:44.559 --> 00:01:46.480
without checking their classes?
